\f1\fs22 \'93Brilliant ideas, people,\'94 Gerry urged. \'93I want brilliant ideas. If we so much as crack any Airborne real estate above that site, we\'92ll all be forcibly retired.\'94\
Silence thickened around the table. Only the sounds of water pouring, gulps, and clicking pens defined its edges and made it deeper.\
\'93Anyone?\'94 Gerry pressed.\
Someone tried. \'93Well, it\'92s... I mean...\'94 They coughed and drank.\
\'93What?\'94\
\'93There\'92s been a development. Since our first recon of the Pale site, a development application\'92s been approved. A religious memorial, apparently.\'94\
Gerry threw up his hands. He knew the routine: an Airborne\'92s notion of Cloudborne philanthropy was to dedicate a park or statue that reminded everyone of their own virtue. Two kinds of Airborne construction ever happened below the Layer\'97tower footings, and \'93Holy Art\'94 glorifying meritocratic doctrine.\
\'93Which saint?\'94\
\'93Saint Zucker.\'94\
\'93Tax,\'94 Gerry muttered\'97the patron of privacy \'93protection\'94 and identity mining, one of the nine major saints of Airborne canon.\
\'93It\'92s not just a statue.\'94\
\'93It never is.\'94\
\'93This one\'92s officially for the Great Viral Cleansing Memorial and Museum.\'94\
\'93So it\'92ll be a front for something else.\'94\
\'93Can we delay construction until we\'92ve investigated the site?\'94\
\'93It\'92s too late. The statue\'92s nearly finished.\'94\
\'93What? It\'92s only six weeks since we cased the place. How could\'97?\'94 But Gerry knew how quickly Airborne saints went from concept to concrete when sanctimonious aggrandisement facilitating the exchange of shekels was involved.\
He ran through the protocol mentally. The statue was always first and guaranteed; as soon as it was up, further construction became untouchable. No one challenged a saint, especially one of the Nine.\
Amid the congealing quiet, Gerry heard faint keyboard tapping. Down at the far end, a junior minion typed furiously, the glow from her oversized utilitarian monitor outlining her hunched shoulders. She raised her hand, hesitantly, as every senior in the room turned to stare.\
\'93Yes?\'94 Gerry barked. \'93What is it?\'94\
\'93I think I might have a solution.\'94\
The minion typed a complicated flurry of commands. A holographic map of the city rose over the table. She zoomed and panned, sometimes stopping to add more data. A pin appeared. \'93Here\'92s our bioweapons bunker,\'94 she said, fingers still racing. \'93And here, less than two blocks away\'85\'94 She paused for the next map layer. \'93... is the site of the Stickett Inn bombing.\'94\
\'93What about it?\'94\
With a few more keystrokes, she zoomed. \'93Demolition is nearly finished. It\'92s literally a big hole in the ground.\'94\
\'93What\'92s happening in the street alongside it?\'94\
\'93The nightclub\'92s still operating, I think.\'94\
\'93Out in the street?\'94\
Across the table, several incredulous attendees muttered \'93Cloudborne,\'94 as if that summed up all necessary context. Nods and grimaces swept the room.\
The minion kept working, drawing another map layer above the first. \'93This shows underground structures at the depth we expect.\'94\
\'93So what?\'94 one impatient attendee bristled, annoyed at a junior grabbing the spotlight.\
Gerry squinted. \'93The sub-basement under the bomb site is about the same depth as the bunker.\'94\
\'93Exactly, sir,\'94 the minion said. Gerry had to make an effort not to beam at the praise. He hid his pride behind a controlled expression.\
\'93There are a few obstacles\'97utility tunnels, pipes, the usual.\'94\
\'93We could dig across from the bomb site to the target,\'94 Gerry said.\
\'93Exactly, sir. That\'92s the idea.\'94\
\'93My idea,\'94 Gerry said. \'93That\'92s my idea.\'94\
The minion nodded obediently as senior staff eyed the exchange with confusion and existential dread.\
\'93Yes, sir. And if I could add, thinking laterally and, in fact, literally laterally, is a mark of true leadership.\'94
